item.text:
**Reused evidence**
No prior reusable evidence IDs were materially applicable to 5B, so this dossier uses fresh 2022 economic sources rather than cross-chapter reuse.

**New evidence**
- [IBGE: GDP grows 2.9% in 2022, closes the year at R$9.9 trillion](https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-press-room/2185-news-agency/releases-en/36377-gdp-grows-2-9-in-2022-closes-the-year-at-r-9-9-trillion) - Brazil’s GDP rose 2.9% in 2022, with services and industry up and agriculture down, showing a solid but not uniformly broad recovery.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"GDP grows 2.9% in 2022, closes the year at R$9.9 trillion","publisher":"IBGE","publication_date":"2023-03-02","url":"https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-press-room/2185-news-agency/releases-en/36377-gdp-grows-2-9-in-2022-closes-the-year-at-r-9-9-trillion","claim":"Brazil's GDP grew 2.9% in 2022, with services up 4.2%, industry up 1.6%, and agriculture down 1.7%.","locator":"Highlights / comparison table","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IBGE is the official national statistics agency and this is a standard GDP release with direct annual estimates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 target year; published March 2023 with the final annual estimate.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual, not uniquely attributable to Bolsonaro; reflects the full-year macro record under his administration plus global and domestic conditions.","contrary_evidence":["Growth remained uneven across sectors.","Does not by itself show ruler-driven prosperity gains."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10","5B.6"]}

- [IBGE: Continuous PNAD 2022 unemployment was 9.3%](https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-press-room/2185-news-agency/releases-en/36358-continuous-pnad-in-2022-average-annual-unemployment-rate-was-9-3-average-annual-underutilization-rate-was-20-8) - Labor-market recovery was real in 2022, but unemployment and underutilization still remained elevated.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Continuous PNAD: in 2022, average annual unemployment rate was 9.3%, average annual underutilization rate was 20.8%","publisher":"IBGE","publication_date":"2023-02-28","url":"https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-press-room/2185-news-agency/releases-en/36358-continuous-pnad-in-2022-average-annual-unemployment-rate-was-9-3-average-annual-underutilization-rate-was-20-8","claim":"The average annual unemployment rate in 2022 was 9.3%, and employed population averaged 98.0 million, the highest annual average in the series.","locator":"Highlights","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IBGE is the official source for labor-market statistics and the release reports the annual series directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 and was published shortly after year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual outcome under Bolsonaro's final year; not a direct policy effect by itself.","contrary_evidence":["High unemployment and underemployment still persisted.","Does not isolate Bolsonaro-specific causation."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10","5B.8"]}

- [IBGE: IPCA closed 2022 at 5.79%](https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-press-room/2185-news-agency/releases-en/36059-ipca-was-0-62-in-december-closes-2022-at-5-79) - Inflation ended the year above the target band, and food and beverages were a major contributor.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IPCA was 0.62% in December, closes 2022 at 5.79%","publisher":"IBGE","publication_date":"2023-01-10","url":"https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-press-room/2185-news-agency/releases-en/36059-ipca-was-0-62-in-december-closes-2022-at-5-79","claim":"Brazil's IPCA rose 5.79% in 2022, and food and beverages were a major contributor to December inflation.","locator":"Highlights / table of group impacts","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IBGE is the official inflation statistician and the release reports the final annual CPI series.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 annual inflation outcome, published January 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual macro outcome under Bolsonaro's final year; inflation was also shaped by global food and fuel shocks and monetary policy.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation fell from 2021's 10.06% rate.","Lower inflation alone did not restore household purchasing power."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.9"]}

- [World Bank: Food Insecurity and Food Inflation in Brazil](https://www.worldbank.org/pt/country/brazil/publication/brazil-food-insecurity-and-food-inflation) - The World Bank estimated severe food insecurity at 30.7% in 2022 and tied the burden to food-price inflation and reliance on transfers.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflação de alimentos e insegurança alimentar no Brasil","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-04-01","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/pt/country/brazil/publication/brazil-food-insecurity-and-food-inflation","claim":"The World Bank estimated that 30.7% of Brazil's population experienced moderate or severe food insecurity in 2022, and said food prices rose 37.5% between end-2019 and end-2022.","locator":"Body text discussing 2022 food insecurity and prices","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank analysis is high-quality and explicitly links 2022 food insecurity to inflation and household welfare.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Explicitly discusses 2022 conditions, with retrospective comparisons to 2019-2021.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual, but relevant to policy because social protection and inflation responses during Bolsonaro's term affected household exposure.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes social-protection measures cushioned the worst pandemic-era losses.","The evidence does not isolate Bolsonaro from inherited pandemic effects."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- [World Bank: Fighting against poverty: the impact of inflation on households’ purchasing power](https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/opinion/2022/10/19/fighting-against-poverty-the-impact-of-inflation-on-households-purchasing-power) - Brazil’s inflation and food-cost surge materially eroded household purchasing power in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fighting against poverty: the impact of inflation on households’ purchasing power","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/opinion/2022/10/19/fighting-against-poverty-the-impact-of-inflation-on-households-purchasing-power","claim":"By mid-2022, annual inflation in Brazil was estimated at 13.9%, and food costs had risen 13.43% in the 12 months to August 2022.","locator":"Opinion text / data paragraph on Brazil","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank staff commentary draws on contemporaneous price data and is directly tied to household welfare.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses 2022 price conditions, published in October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual; reflects inflation that Bolsonaro's government tried to offset but did not fully control.","contrary_evidence":["Nominal average labor incomes also rose, partly offsetting the shock.","The piece is analytic commentary, not a ruler-specific audit."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.9"]}

- [BCB: Our Results 2022](https://www.bcb.gov.br/en/publications/our_results_2022) - Brazil missed its 2022 inflation target, and the central bank says it issued an open letter explaining why.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Our Results","publisher":"Banco Central do Brasil","publication_date":"2023-01-01","url":"https://www.bcb.gov.br/en/publications/our_results_2022","claim":"For 2022, Brazil's inflation target was 3.5% with a 1.5-point tolerance band, but actual inflation was 5.79%, triggering an open letter on the breach.","locator":"Annual report section on inflation and purchasing power","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The central bank is the authoritative source for the target framework and annual inflation breach.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 and published as the annual report for that year.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual and partly attributable to the administration's policy environment; the BCB itself is institutionally independent.","contrary_evidence":["The inflation-targeting framework itself was inherited and independent of Bolsonaro.","This does not prove presidential mismanagement on its own."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

- [BCB Copom minutes, March 15-16 2022](https://www.bcb.gov.br/en/publications/copomminutes/16032022) - The central bank cited the Ukraine war, commodity shock, and fiscal risk premia while continuing to tighten rates.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Minutes of the Monetary Policy Committee — Copom","publisher":"Banco Central do Brasil","publication_date":"2022-05-16","url":"https://www.bcb.gov.br/en/publications/copomminutes/16032022","claim":"Copom said the Russia-Ukraine war worsened inflationary pressures, warned that fiscal policies that worsen the future fiscal path could raise risk premia and de-anchor expectations, and kept tightening monetary policy.","locator":"Sections A, B, and D; paragraphs 1, 11, 12, 21-24","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Copom minutes are the primary record of Brazil's monetary-policy deliberations and the inflation-risk assessment in March 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and highly relevant to the macro environment Bolsonaro faced.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual, because the central bank is independent; relevant mainly as a contemporaneous assessment of the policy environment Bolsonaro was governing.","contrary_evidence":["The tightening cycle was executed by the independent central bank, not the president alone.","The minutes do not assign responsibility to Bolsonaro personally."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

- [BCB Copom minutes, June 15 2022](https://www.bcb.gov.br/publicacoes/atascopom/15062022) - Growth and employment were recovering, but inflation remained broad-based and driven by food and fuel.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Atas do Comitê de Política Monetária - Copom","publisher":"Banco Central do Brasil","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://www.bcb.gov.br/publicacoes/atascopom/15062022","claim":"Copom said Brazilian activity was growing above expectations and the labor market was recovering, but inflation remained high and broad-based, with food and fuel shocks still prominent.","locator":"Paragraphs 4-5","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary central-bank minutes documenting the state of the 2022 economy and inflation drivers.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in target year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than directly attributable to Bolsonaro, but relevant to the administration's economic environment and response choices.","contrary_evidence":["The central bank attributes some weakness to global and commodity shocks.","Positive output and labor recovery complicate any one-direction verdict."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- [BCB Copom minutes, October 25-26 2022](https://www.bcb.gov.br/en/publications/copomminutes/26102022) - Late-2022 inflation was partially pulled down by tax measures and cheaper fuel, while underlying inflation still stayed elevated.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Minutes of the Monetary Policy Committee — Copom","publisher":"Banco Central do Brasil","publication_date":"2022-11-01","url":"https://www.bcb.gov.br/en/publications/copomminutes/26102022","claim":"Copom said the labor market continued to recover, consumer inflation had recently been reduced by tax measures and lower fuel prices, but underlying inflation remained above the range compatible with the inflation target.","locator":"Paragraphs 1-5 and 21-22","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"This is the primary monetary-policy record for October 2022 and directly documents the inflation dynamic.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual; the tax measures were part of the Bolsonaro government's policy mix, while monetary policy was independent.","contrary_evidence":["Tax-driven disinflation can be temporary and fiscally costly.","Underlying inflation still remained elevated."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

- [Brazil Congress approves major spending bill, lifting Bolsonaro re-election hopes](https://www.investing.com/news/economy/brazil-congress-approves-major-spending-bill-lifting-bolsonaro-reelection-hopes-2847009) - The administration backed a pre-election spending package that bypassed the cap and raised concerns about inflation and fiscal discipline.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil Congress approves major spending bill, lifting Bolsonaro re-election hopes","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-07-13","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/brazil-congress-approves-major-spending-bill-lifting-bolsonaro-reelection-hopes-2847009","claim":"Congress approved a spending package Bolsonaro was banking on for reelection that bypassed the spending cap and boosted social benefits, prompting investor concern about fiscal discipline and inflation.","locator":"Lead paragraph and package description","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is strong independent coverage, though the piece relies partly on market expectations and political context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct enough to Bolsonaro's reelection strategy because the package was explicitly tied to his campaign and administration.","contrary_evidence":["Government said it needed urgent relief for households hit by inflation.","The package had congressional approval, not sole presidential unilateral action."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.5","5B.9"]}

- [Brazil's benefits spree and Bolsonaro promises cost R$273 billion in 2022-2023](https://noticias.uol.com.br/ultimas-noticias/reuters/2022/10/21/saiba-mais-beneficios-em-serie-e-promessas-de-bolsonaro-em-ano-eleitoral-tem-custo-de-r273-bi-em-2022-e-2023.htm) - Reuters calculated a very large election-year fiscal expansion, much of it already enacted.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"SAIBA MAIS-Benefícios em série e promessas de Bolsonaro em ano eleitoral têm custo de R$273 bi em 2022 e 2023","publisher":"UOL / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://noticias.uol.com.br/ultimas-noticias/reuters/2022/10/21/saiba-mais-beneficios-em-serie-e-promessas-de-bolsonaro-em-ano-eleitoral-tem-custo-de-r273-bi-em-2022-e-2023.htm","claim":"Reuters estimated that Bolsonaro's 2022 election-year benefits and promises would cost R$273 billion in 2022-2023, with R$127.4 billion already approved and implemented.","locator":"Lead and calculation summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is a strong independent source, and the UOL mirror preserves the original report and numbers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and tied to the election period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Bolsonaro's election strategy and executive choices.","contrary_evidence":["Some measures were already approved through Congress, not by executive fiat alone.","Short-term relief can be justified in inflationary periods, but fiscal sustainability is the counterpoint."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.5","5B.8"]}

- [Bolsonaro expected deflation from lower fuel prices after ICMS changes](https://noticias.uol.com.br/ultimas-noticias/reuters/2022/07/14/bolsonaro-diz-esperar-deflacao-em-agosto-com-queda-no-preco-dos-combustiveis.htm) - He openly treated fuel-tax relief as an anti-inflation policy and claimed it would produce deflation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bolsonaro diz esperar deflação em agosto com queda no preço dos combustíveis","publisher":"UOL / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-07-14","url":"https://noticias.uol.com.br/ultimas-noticias/reuters/2022/07/14/bolsonaro-diz-esperar-deflacao-em-agosto-com-queda-no-preco-dos-combustiveis.htm","claim":"Bolsonaro said he expected deflation the following month because lower fuel prices after an ICMS tax change would reduce inflation.","locator":"Quoted remarks in the Reuters report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters captured the president's own public statement, which is reliable for the claim he made even if not for its predictive accuracy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential rhetoric and policy framing.","contrary_evidence":["The effect was framed as temporary and dependent on tax changes.","Fuel-tax relief can also be fiscally costly and not structurally anti-inflationary."],"lenses":["5B.7","5B.9","5B.3"]}

- [Bolsonaro blamed governors and Ukraine for inflation](https://economia.uol.com.br/noticias/reuters/2022/03/22/bolsonaro-reconhece-inflacao-mas-volta-a-culpar-governadores.htm) - He acknowledged inflation but shifted responsibility away from his own government.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bolsonaro reconhece inflação, mas culpa estados e guerra na Ucrânia","publisher":"UOL / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://economia.uol.com.br/noticias/reuters/2022/03/22/bolsonaro-reconhece-inflacao-mas-volta-a-culpar-governadores.htm","claim":"Bolsonaro acknowledged inflation in food and fuel but blamed state governors and the war in Ukraine for the price surge.","locator":"Quoted remarks in the Reuters report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is reliable for contemporaneous public statements and the piece directly captures Bolsonaro's rhetoric.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential rhetoric, relevant to honesty and accountability for shocks.","contrary_evidence":["Ukraine war and global commodity shocks were real external drivers.","The statement alone does not prove policy failure or success."],"lenses":["5B.7","5B.9","5B.1"]}

- [Bolsonaro delegated budget-credit authority to Economy Minister Paulo Guedes](https://www.gov.br/secretariageral/pt-br/noticias/2022/janeiro/decreto-preve-delegacao-de-competencias-ao-ministro-de-estado-da-economia) - This is one of the clearer signs that he empowered a technocratic economic minister on budget execution.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Decreto prevê delegação de competências ao Ministro de Estado da Economia","publisher":"Secretaria-Geral da Presidência / Governo do Brasil","publication_date":"2022-01-13","url":"https://www.gov.br/secretariageral/pt-br/noticias/2022/janeiro/decreto-preve-delegacao-de-competencias-ao-ministro-de-estado-da-economia","claim":"Bolsonaro issued a decree delegating to the Economy Minister authority over opening 2022 budget credits and related budget adjustments to speed execution.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"This is the official government announcement of an enacted decree with a clear administrative delegation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct and strong: this was a presidential decree under Bolsonaro's authority.","contrary_evidence":["Delegation does not prove the minister was competent, only that he was empowered.","Budget execution still faced fiscal and inflationary constraints."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.6"]}

- [Bolsonaro extended payroll-tax relief to 17 sectors](https://www.gov.br/secretariageral/pt-br/noticias/2022/janeiro/presidente-bolsonaro-sanciona-projeto-de-lei-da-desoneracao-da-folha-de-pagamento-de-17-setores-da-economia/) - The administration used sectoral tax relief as a job-creation policy, with mixed efficiency and distribution implications.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Presidente Bolsonaro sanciona Projeto de Lei da desoneração da folha de pagamento de 17 setores da economia","publisher":"Secretaria-Geral da Presidência / Governo do Brasil","publication_date":"2022-01-03","url":"https://www.gov.br/secretariageral/pt-br/noticias/2022/janeiro/presidente-bolsonaro-sanciona-projeto-de-lei-da-desoneracao-da-folha-de-pagamento-de-17-setores-da-economia/","claim":"Bolsonaro signed a law extending payroll-tax relief through 2023 for 17 labor-intensive sectors, including construction, transport, and communications.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official record of the enacted measure and its stated policy purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, though the law was signed at the start of the year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential action, though the measure originated in Congress and was framed as a jobs policy.","contrary_evidence":["Sectoral tax relief can privilege connected industries rather than broad-based reform.","The measure creates revenue costs that have to be offset elsewhere."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.1"]}

- [PPI investment pipeline exceeded R$1.3 trillion](https://www.gov.br/en/government-of-brazil/latest-news/2022/brazils-investments-will-exceed-brl-1-3-trillion-in-the-coming-years) - The administration framed its infrastructure program as a growth engine and reported substantial private investment commitments.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil's investments will exceed BRL 1.3 trillion in the coming years","publisher":"Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-03-17","url":"https://www.gov.br/en/government-of-brazil/latest-news/2022/brazils-investments-will-exceed-brl-1-3-trillion-in-the-coming-years","claim":"The government said the Investment Partnership Program would invest BRL 78 billion in 2022 and that commitments signed between 2019 and 2022 would exceed BRL 1.3 trillion over coming years.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official program summary is reliable for what the administration claimed and what commitments it reported, though not all downstream effects are independently verified here.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, though partly forward-looking.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Bolsonaro's administration and its investment policy agenda.","contrary_evidence":["This is a pipeline/commitment figure, not a measured welfare outcome.","Long-run effectiveness depends on execution and project quality."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

- [PPI concluded 48 projects and secured R$101 billion in 2022](https://www.gov.br/pt-br/noticias/financas-impostos-e-gestao-publica/2022/12/secretaria-especial-do-ppi-concluiu-48-projetos-em-2022-e-assegurou-r-101-bilhoes-em-novos-investimentos/) - This is stronger implementation evidence than the pipeline projection alone.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Secretaria Especial do PPI concluiu 48 projetos em 2022 e assegurou R$ 101 bilhões em novos investimentos","publisher":"Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-12-26","url":"https://www.gov.br/pt-br/noticias/financas-impostos-e-gestao-publica/2022/12/secretaria-especial-do-ppi-concluiu-48-projetos-em-2022-e-assegurou-r-101-bilhoes-em-novos-investimentos/","claim":"The PPI said it concluded 48 projects in 2022 and secured more than R$101 billion in contracted investments, including the Eletrobras capitalisation and airport concessions.","locator":"Lead paragraphs and year-end summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official year-end program report on completed projects and contracted investment, with clear implementation language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Bolsonaro administration's investment-policy execution, though project-level economics depend on private actors and regulators.","contrary_evidence":["Contracted investments are not the same as realized household welfare gains.","Infrastructure gains are long run and uneven."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

- [Plano Safra 2022/23 made R$340.88 billion in rural credit available](https://www.gov.br/agricultura/pt-br/assuntos/politica-agricola/plano-safra/2022-2023/2022-2023) - The government expanded agricultural credit materially, which supports productive capacity but also favors a specific sector.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022/2023","publisher":"Ministry of Agriculture and Livestock / Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.gov.br/agricultura/pt-br/assuntos/politica-agricola/plano-safra/2022-2023/2022-2023","claim":"The government said Plano Safra 2022/23 would provide R$340.88 billion in rural credit, up 36% from the previous plan.","locator":"Main summary block","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"This is the official launch page for the 2022/23 rural credit program, with the stated amount and percentage increase.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct government policy under Bolsonaro, though sectoral benefits are concentrated in agriculture rather than the whole economy.","contrary_evidence":["Sectoral credit expansion can help productivity but does not prove broad household welfare gains.","Agricultural output can also be exposed to commodity and weather cycles."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- [Treasury: federal government posted a primary surplus in 2022](https://www.gov.br/tesouronacional/pt-br/noticias/governo-federal-apresenta-superavit-primario-de-r-59-7-bilhoes-em-2022/) - The year ended with a positive primary result, but it sits alongside election-year spending and inflationary pressures.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Governo Federal apresenta superávit primário de R$ 59,7 bilhões em 2022","publisher":"National Treasury / Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2023-03-01","url":"https://www.gov.br/tesouronacional/pt-br/noticias/governo-federal-apresenta-superavit-primario-de-r-59-7-bilhoes-em-2022/","claim":"The federal government reported a 2022 primary surplus of R$59.7 billion, above the year's formal projection.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National Treasury is the authoritative source for the official fiscal outcome and the release gives the final figure directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022, published after year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Bolsonaro administration's fiscal choices, but the result also reflects one-offs, transfers, and revenue conditions beyond his control.","contrary_evidence":["A surplus does not erase election-year spending expansion or inflation pressure.","The headline result may not reflect underlying sustainability or quality of spending."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 30 documents across IBGE, BCB, World Bank, Treasury, Planalto/gov.br, Reuters/UOL, IMF, and PPI pages.

Retained sources were chosen because they added distinct evidence rather than repeating the same macro point:
- Official macro series from IBGE for growth, labor, and inflation.
- Central-bank minutes and annual report for inflation-target breach and policy reaction.
- World Bank material for household welfare, food insecurity, and inflation transmission.
- Treasury and Planalto/gov.br releases for enacted fiscal/investment measures.
- Reuters syndications for election-year spending, fuel-tax framing, and scapegoating rhetoric.

Rejected material fell into these buckets:
- Duplicate Reuters republications of the same story from multiple outlets.
- Generic 2023/2024 Brazil macro summaries that did not add new 2022-specific facts.
- Purely political coverage with no economic-policy content.
- Forward-looking or promotional government pages that stated policy intent but did not add a distinct 2022 implementation or outcome claim.
- Post-2022 coup / election-denial coverage that was outside the target year unless explicitly used only as retrospective context, which I did not rely on here.

**Remaining gaps**
- 5B.2 is still only partially covered. I have one strong direct delegation measure for Paulo Guedes, but not a full appointment/performance audit of the economic team.
- 5B.5 is still thinner than the macro sections. I have evidence of sectoral privileges and election-year spending, but not a deep anti-corruption or capture record for the economic portfolio.
- 5B.8 would benefit from more household-distribution detail by region, class, gender, and race beyond the World Bank’s food-insecurity and poverty material.
- More search on implementation quality for the infrastructure and privatization programs would help separate real productivity gains from announcement-heavy policy.
